SUMMARY:History Panel: "Accounting for Power: New Histories of Capitalism 
 and the State"
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261002T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261002T173000
DTSTAMP:20260924T032428Z
UID:dartmouth-82746@free-food-dartmouth
SEQUENCE:0
CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:Original event page(s):\nhttps://home.dartmouth.edu/events/eve
 nt?event=82746\nhttps://www.benchansfield.com/\nhttps://wwnorton.com/books
 /9781324093510\nhttps://history.yale.edu/people/emilie-connolly\nhttps://p
 ress.princeton.edu/books/hardcover/9780691240121/vested-interests?srsltid=
 AU7gw4V4dwn45bRU2aMa6VRxm4KFiQkZ4LcvVgzAJUN-v1aWtHdb3VNh\n\nDescription:\n
 Join the Department of History in welcoming a panel of two Dartmouth Histo
 ry post-doctoral alumni\, authors of recent award-winning books in the his
 tory of capitalism that were their research while at Dartmouth. Reception 
 and refreshments to follow. Followed by a reception\, with free books for 
 the first 30 student attendees\, with more for sale following the panel co
 urtsey of Norwich Bookstore.\nBench Ansfield\nAssistant Professor of Histo
 ry\, TEMPLE UNIVERSITY\nAuthor of\nBorn in Flames: The Business of Arson a
 nd the Remaking of the American City\n(W.W.Norton\, 2025)\nFinalist for th
 e\nPulitzer Prize in History\nWinner of the\nLos Angeles Times Book Prize 
 for History\nWinner of the\nAnisfield-Wolf Book Award in Nonfiction\nWinne
 r of the\nFrancis Parkman Prize for the Best Book in American History from
  the Society of American Historians\nWinner of the\nFrederick Jackson Turn
 er Award from the Organization of American Historians\nWinner of the\nNew 
 York City Book Award\nWinner of the\nAthenaeum of Philadelphia Literary Aw
 ard\nWinner of the\nBancroft Prize in American History\nWinner of the\nJam
 es A. Rawley Award\, Organization of American Historians\nFinalist for the
 \nHagley Prize\, Business History Conference\nWinner of the\nStuart L. Ber
 nath Book Prize\, Society for Historians of American Foreign Relations\nEm
 ilie Connolly\nAssistant Professor of History\, YALE UNIVERSITY\nAuthor of
 \nVested Interests: Trusteeship and Native Dispossession in the United Sta
 tes\n(Princeton University Press\, 2025)\nWinner of the\nBancroft Prize in
  American History\nWinner of the\nJames A. Rawley Award\, Organization of 
 American Historians\nFinalist for the\nHagley Prize\, Business History Con
 ference\nWinner of the\nStuart L. Bernath Book Prize\, Society for Histori
 ans of American Foreign Relations\n\nSponsor / organizer: Geography Depart
 ment\, History Department\, Native American and Indigenous Studies\, Socio
 logy Department\n\nAudience: Public\n\nSource: Dartmouth\n\nWhy this was i
 ncluded: food keyword near service context\n\nFood availability is inferre
 d from the source listing\; verify details before attending.
