BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Biomedical Data Science Seminar with Xiao Wu\, PhD - "Causal Machi
 ne Learning Methods to Assess Long-Term Impacts of Extreme Weather Events 
 and Policies"
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261008T110000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261008T120000
DTSTAMP:20260924T032428Z
UID:geisel-6248-2026-10-8@free-food-dartmouth
SEQUENCE:0
CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:Original event page(s):\nhttps://geiselmed.dartmouth.edu/calen
 dar/event_view.php?eid=6248&instance=2026-10-8\n\nDescription:\nThe Depart
 ment of Biomedical Data Science invites you to attend a seminar in person
  on Thursday\, October 8 from 11:00-12:00pm at DHMC\, Auditorium H with Xi
 ao Wu\, PhD\, Assistant Professor of Biostatistics\, Columbia University.\
 nTalk title: “Causal Machine Learning Methods to Assess Long-Term Impact
 s of Extreme Weather Events and Policies”\nLight refreshments will be pr
 ovided on a first-come\, first-served basis.\nThe presentation will take p
 lace both in person at DHMC\, Auditorium H (and via Zoom) and we encourage
  as many of you to attend in person as possible. Please write to biomedica
 l.data.science@dartmouth.edu to request the Zoom login details.\nPlease se
 e description below for more details.\nPlease invite your fellow faculty c
 olleagues\, research associates\, graduate trainees\, and post-docs!\nPres
 entation Summary\nExtreme weather events\, including wildfires and tropica
 l cyclones\, are becoming more frequent and intense\, yet substantial gaps
  remain in understanding how their health and social consequences evolve o
 ver multiple years in affected communities. This talk introduces covariate
 -balanced synthetic control\, a new causal inference approach that connect
 s synthetic control with covariate balancing methods and incorporates tail
 ored balance conditions to accommodate high-dimensional pre-exposure covar
 iates\, improving the feasibility and computational efficiency of estimati
 on in large-scale environmental data. Dr. Wu will illustrate the approach 
 through two applications: an analysis of satellite-based fire activity acr
 oss California forests from 2000 to 2021\, which found that low-intensity 
 fires substantially reduce the risk of subsequent high-intensity wildfires
 \, and an analysis of U.S. tropical cyclones from 2005 to 2018\, which cha
 racterized changes in social vulnerability.\nBiography\nXiao Wu\, PhD\, is
  an Assistant Professor of Biostatistics at Columbia University and a Heal
 th AI Scientist at Meta. His research develops statistical\, machine learn
 ing\, and causal inference methods to address methodological challenges in
  health research\, with particular emphasis on generating evidence and pol
 icy solutions to mitigate the health impacts of environmental and climate-
 related exposures. He is also interested in health technologies and AI eva
 luation for health applications. He earned his Ph.D. in Biostatistics from
  Harvard University and completed a Data Science Postdoctoral Fellowship a
 t Stanford University. Named to the Forbes 30 Under 30 list\, he has publi
 shed in Science\, the New England Journal of Medicine\, The Lancet Planeta
 ry Health\, and the Journal of the American Statistical Association\, and 
 his work has been featured by international media including The New York T
 imes\, National Geographic\, Scientific American\, and the Financial Times
 .\n\nSponsor / organizer: Biomedical Data Science (  )\n\nSource: Geisel\n
 \nWhy this was included: explicit food-service wording\; food keyword near
  service context\n\nFood availability is inferred from the source listing\
 ; verify details before attending.
LAST-MODIFIED:20260924T032428Z
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
URL:https://geiselmed.dartmouth.edu/calendar/event_view.php?eid=6248&insta
 nce=2026-10-8
